---
import { supportEmail } from "../config/contact";
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
---

<LegalLayout
    title="Support"
    description="Help with accounts, events, profiles, privacy controls, safety tools, and the live nearby-discovery experience."
    lastUpdated="August 31, 2026"
>
    <section>
        <h2>Getting started</h2>
        <p>
            AReach is used inside closed professional events. Create an event
            and share its invite code with guests, or join an existing event
            with the code provided by the event host.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Account and privacy</h2>

        <h3>How do I delete my account?</h3>
        <p>
            In AReach, open <strong>Profile → Account → Delete Account</strong>.
            Confirm with your current password and then confirm the permanent
            deletion prompt. Account deletion cannot be undone; email is not
            required for the normal deletion flow.
        </p>

        <h3>How do I hide my profile from nearby attendees?</h3>
        <p>
            Open <strong>Profile → Privacy</strong> and turn{" "}
            <strong>Visible to nearby attendees</strong> off. You remain a
            participant in your events and can still see and edit your own
            profile, but your AReach identity and profile are hidden from other
            attendees in attendee lists, nearby results, distance displays,
            and camera cards. Event counts may still include you, and the event
            host cannot override the setting.
        </p>

        <h3>How do I report an attendee?</h3>
        <p>
            During a live event, open the attendee’s profile, open the{" "}
            <strong>More attendee actions</strong> menu (⋯), and choose{" "}
            <strong>Report attendee</strong>. Select the reason that best
            describes the issue and choose <strong>Submit Report</strong>.
            Available reasons include inappropriate profile content, harassment
            or abusive behavior, impersonation, spam, and other.
        </p>

        <h3>How do I block or unblock an attendee?</h3>
        <p>
            During a live event, open the attendee’s profile, open the{" "}
            <strong>More attendee actions</strong> menu (⋯), and choose{" "}
            <strong>Block attendee</strong>. Confirm the prompt. AReach then
            hides the two users from each other’s attendee discovery, profiles,
            nearby results, distances, and camera cards. To unblock someone,
            open <strong>Profile → Blocked Users</strong> and choose{" "}
            <strong>Unblock</strong>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Events and nearby discovery</h2>

        <h3>How do I create an event?</h3>
        <p>
            Open the Create flow, provide the event title, description, date,
            time, venue, and capacity, then complete the event setup. Share the
            generated invite code with attendees.
        </p>

        <h3>How do I join an event?</h3>
        <p>
            From the app’s join flow, enter the invite code shared by the host.
            You must be authenticated, and the event must still be available
            for registration.
        </p>

        <h3>How does nearby discovery work?</h3>
        <p>
            During a live event, AReach can use the camera, ARKit, Bluetooth,
            Local Network services, and Apple Nearby Interaction to show eligible
            nearby attendees on a compatible iPhone. Both people need to be
            participating in the same live event, and the relevant permissions
            and device capabilities must be available. Nearby discovery is not
            a GPS or general people-finding service.
        </p>

        <h3>Why can’t I see an attendee or camera overlay?</h3>
        <p>
            Check that you are in the same live event and have joined or checked
            in. In iOS Settings, allow AReach the Camera, Bluetooth, Local
            Network, and Nearby Interaction access requested by the live
            feature. Also check that the other attendee has not turned off{" "}
            <strong>Visible to nearby attendees</strong> and that you do not
            have a blocking relationship. Camera conditions, AR tracking,
            device compatibility, distance, and the other device’s current
            availability can also affect overlays.
        </p>
        <p>
            AReach intentionally does not reveal whether a particular attendee
            is hidden because of a privacy setting, blocking, event state, or
            temporary availability. Refresh the event or leave and re-enter the
            live experience after checking the conditions above.
        </p>

        <h3>How do I update my profile?</h3>
        <p>
            Open Profile and choose <strong>Edit Profile</strong>. You can
            update your name, headline, and “Ask me about…” topics. These
            conversation cues are shown only in event contexts where your
            attendee profile is visible.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Contact and safety</h2>
        <p>
            For technical support, privacy questions, access or correction
            requests, or safety concerns that you cannot report in the app,
            email <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a>. Include
            enough detail for us to understand the issue, but never send your
            password or authentication codes.
        </p>
        <p>
            If you are in immediate danger, contact local emergency services.
            The in-app report and block tools are not emergency services.
        </p>
        <p>
            Read the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> for data practices
            and the <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a> for the rules
            governing use of AReach.
        </p>
    </section>
</LegalLayout>
